## Streams were introduced in Java 8 to provide a declarative, functional, and composable approach to processing sequences of data
    ## Before Streams, developers had to rely on imperative loops (for or while) and temporary collections to filter, transform, or
        ## aggregate data. This often resulted in verbose, boilerplate-heavy code that was difficult to read, maintain, and parallelise

## Core Reasons for Introducing Streams
__ 
Core Reasons for Introducing Streams

1. Declarative vs. Imperative Processing: Instead of writing how to do something (loops, index tracking, conditions), 
      Streams allow you to express what you want to achieve (e.g., filter this, map that).

2. Readability & Boilerplate Reduction: Complex data manipulation pipelines can be written in just a few lines of clean, readable code.

3. Lazy Evaluation: Intermediate operations (like filter or map) are not executed immediately. 
      They are optimized and combined into a single-pass execution pipeline that only triggers when a terminal operation
          (like collect or forEach) is invoked. As pointed out by a consensus of users on Stack Overflow, this real-time pipelining allows
                individual items to move through a chain of functions sequentially without waiting for the entire collection to finish previous stages.


4. No Structural Modifications: Streams process data from a source (like a List) 
        without altering the original data structure, preventing accidental side effects.

5. Effortless Parallelism: You can easily leverage multi-core processors by switching from .
      stream() to .parallelStream(), avoiding complex manual thread management.
